<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>netstandard2.0</TargetFramework>
    <IncludeBuildOutput>false</IncludeBuildOutput>
    <GenerateDependencyFile>false</GenerateDependencyFile>
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);NU5128;NU5100</NoWarn>
    <PackageId>TINS.Native.Desktop</PackageId>
    <Description>Meta-package bundling TINS.Native.win-x64, TINS.Native.linux-x64, and TINS.Native.osx-arm64, so a multi-platform consumer (e.g. a cross-targeted test project) can pull in native OpenBLAS/PocketFFT assets for every desktop RID with a single reference instead of adding each RID package individually. A single-RID deployment should still reference just its own TINS.Native.&lt;rid&gt; package directly -- this carries no native content of its own. osx-x64 is deliberately excluded, see the PackageReference comment below. FFTW is not included here, per the FFTW/core license split (GPL-2.0-or-later vs. BSD-3-Clause) -- see TINS.Native.FFTW.Desktop for the equivalent opt-in bundle.</Description>
    <PackageTags>Neuroscience, Native, OpenBLAS, PocketFFT, Desktop</PackageTags>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- $(Version) isn't usable here: MinVer computes it via a build target that runs
         *after* restore, but restore needs a concrete version right now to resolve these
         PackageReferences (chicken and egg).

         This has been through two earlier iterations:
         1. A floating version ("0.0.0-alpha.1.*"): has no publishable meaning in a
            nuspec, so `dotnet pack` collapsed it to whatever concrete sibling version
            resolved *at pack time* and baked THAT in as the minimum, ratcheting up on every
            repack. Once an older sibling version got pruned from a downstream feed, this
            risked a genuine hard restore failure (floor higher than what that feed had).
         2. An abstract, height-less floor ("0.0.0-alpha.1"): never ratchets, but a
            literal "0.0.0-alpha.1" build will never exist, so NU1603 ("dependency version
            not found, nearest match used") fired on *every single restore*, everywhere,
            permanently. Benign, but noisy enough in practice (reported from a real
            downstream consumer, AxoSync) that it's worth avoiding for a floor we can
            actually pin correctly.

         Settled on pinning to "0.0.0-alpha.1.14" specifically: the one height at which
         all four sibling packages have actually been built and sit in the local feed
         together. As a plain (non-floating, non-bracketed) version string this is still a
         minimum-inclusive bound (">= 0.0.0-alpha.1.14"), not an exact pin, so any later
         height still satisfies it once repacked; but a NuGet global-package-cache with an
         older cached copy (e.g. 1.12) no longer silently satisfies it either, forcing
         restore to reach for the real current version instead (this is what was actually
         happening: AxoSync's cache had stale 1.12/1.2 copies that satisfied the old
         "anything >= 0.0.0-alpha.1" floor, not a feed content problem).

         Trade-off, accepted deliberately: this floor needs bumping by hand (edit here, then
         repack) whenever the sibling packages move to a new height that should become the
         new baseline: back to needing a manual step, but only when actually intended,
         not silently on every incidental repack. Update this (and
         MinVerDefaultPreReleaseIdentifiers in Directory.Build.props) together if that
         identifier ever changes (e.g. once real release tags start).

         Bumped win-x64/linux-x64/osx-arm64 to "0.0.0-alpha.1.22" (2026-09-23, after the FFTW/core
         split + pocketfft shim landed and ran green in CI): the new height all three actually
         built RIDs share.

         TINS.Native.osx-x64 was dropped entirely (2026-09-24, while building the real nuget.org
         publish workflow; see NUGET-PUBLISH-CHECKLIST.md, local-only): it used to sit here pinned
         to the old "0.0.0-alpha.1.14" placeholder height, but osx-x64 is now permanently EXCLUDED
         (see CLAUDE.md's Status section), meaning no TINS.Native.osx-x64 package will ever actually
         be published. A PackageReference to a package that doesn't exist on the target feed doesn't
         just degrade for osx-x64 users; it fails restore for this ENTIRE meta-package, for every
         consumer on every platform. Don't add it back unless osx-x64 gets a real (or at least
         actually-published) build of its own.

         Bumped to "1.0.0" (2026-09-24, fixing a real bug): the v1.0.0 tag/publish shipped the
         siblings at clean "1.0.0" but this floor was left at the pre-tag "0.0.0-alpha.1.22",
         producing NU1603 ("dependency version not found, nearest match used") on every consumer
         restore; cosmetic since NuGet still resolved up to the real 1.0.0 correctly, but exactly
         the stale-floor failure mode this pinning design exists to prevent, and permanent noise
         until fixed. This package's own version moves to 1.0.1 (next tag) to publish the fix; this
         floor points at 1.0.0, the actual current sibling version, not 1.0.1; the siblings
         themselves aren't changing, only these two Desktop packages are. -->
    <PackageReference Include="TINS.Native.win-x64" Version="1.0.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="TINS.Native.linux-x64" Version="1.0.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="TINS.Native.osx-arm64" Version="1.0.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
